      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Did I wake you?' Vika asks this with a single word meaning 'to wake up', using rising intonation to turn it into a quick question.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "max",
      "t": "Прости меня, пожалуйста.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Please forgive me.' Max uses the imperative command form of the verb meaning 'sorry'. Then Vika responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Где ты вообще был?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Where were you, anyway?' Vika links 'where' directly with 'you' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "max",
      "t": "Что тут допрос такой?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What is this, an interrogation?' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'what' with 'such'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ты считаешь, что можешь шляться где угодно?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You think you can wander off anywhere?' The reflexive ending on the verb for 'wander' turns the action back onto the subject.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "И приходить в три часа?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And stroll in at three o'clock?' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'and' with 'hours'. Then Max responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "max",
      "t": "Без пятнадцати.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Quarter to.' Max links 'without' directly with 'fifteen' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Что у нас за отношения?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What kind of relationship do we have?' Vika links 'what' directly with 'at' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "max",
      "t": "У нас свободные отношения.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We have an open relationship.' Notice how 'at' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'relations'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "max",
      "t": "Все хорошо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Everything is fine.' Max combines 'all' with 'good' in a natural Russian turn of phrase. Then Vika responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Раз у нас свободные отношения,",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
